    "result_text": "The build is green. Here's a summary of what I implemented.\n\n## Change: string `membershipTier` \u2192 numeric `membershipLevel`\n\nThe old `membershipTier` was a read-only string (`GOLD`/`SILVER`/`BRONZE`) derived on read in the MapStruct mapper. I replaced it with a numeric `membershipLevel` (1\u20133) using the specified rule and removed the tier entirely.\n\n**Level rule** (`MembershipLevels.forOwner`): start at 1, +1 when an email is present, +1 when `namesakeCount` is 0, capped at 3 \u2014 level 4 reserved for tenure. This drops the old `householdSize >= 3 \u2192 GOLD` behaviour, as the spec requires.\n\n**Files changed:**\n\n1. **`mapper/MembershipLevels.java`** (new) \u2014 shared helper computing the level, mirroring the existing `Localities` helper so the mapper and the audit line agree.\n2. **`mapper/OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 replaced the `membershipTier` expression mapping with a `membershipLevel` mapping delegating to `MembershipLevels.forOwner`.\n3. **`resources/openapi.yml`** \u2014 swapped the `membershipTier` (string) schema property for `membershipLevel` (integer, 1\u20133, read-only). Since `OwnerDto` is generated from this spec at build time, this removes `membershipTier` from the response and adds `membershipLevel`.\n4. **`rest/function/owner/AuditOwnerCreated.java`** \u2014 the create audit line now records `membershipLevel={}` (verified in the log output: `AUDIT - Owner created id=11 ... membershipLevel=3`), per \"anything that recorded the tier ... must record the level instead.\"\n5. **`rest/function/owner/AssignHouseholdSize.java`** \u2014 removed the now-stale Javadoc line about promoting households to the GOLD tier.\n\n**Tests:** `./mvnw -o test` \u2192 `BUILD SUCCESS`, `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0`. The `coreNumericLevel` acceptance test (create owner with email + unique name \u2192 `GET` returns `membershipLevel = 3`) passes.\n\nOne judgment call worth flagging: I kept the level **derived on read** (as the tier was) rather than persisting a new DB column. Because its inputs (`email`, `namesakeCount`) are fixed at create time and this app only exposes create/read, the derived value equals the create-time value \u2014 satisfying \"on creation\" without a four-dialect schema migration. If you'd prefer it stored as a real column on `owners`, I can switch to an `AssignMembershipLevel` pipeline step instead.",
